e 31, with several costly moves in a row. The 18k gave a lot back at once, though, and E9 on move 64 turned a -3.7 position into +10.5. The rest was quiet filling in, and the lead stayed near +11.", "moments": [{"turn": 11, "text": "Opus 5.5's G4 took the lead from +0.4 to -4.1, a 4.5-point loss. The engine preferred D3, and the 18k took that point on the next move. It was the first real crack in an even game."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "Opus 5.5 played F5 and lost 13.6 points, dropping from -7.8 to -21.5. The engine wanted D2 on the bottom. By then Black had a string of stones around C3 to D4 and C5 pressed against White's wall, and the move did nothing for them."}, {"turn": 27, "text": "B5 cost 19.7 points, taking Black from -6.6 to -26.3. White's F9 on the move before had just lost 15.7, so this was a straight give-back. The engine's choice was C1, the point Opus 5.5 only took two turns later."}, {"turn": 32, "text": "Opus 5.5's B3 on move 31 had pushed the lead to -26.4, a 20.5-point loss. The 18k answered with D1, which lost 21.6 and moved the score back to -4.7. The engine's choice was A4. The two moves roughly cancelled, and Black was back in the game."}, {"turn": 64, "text": "E9 was the decisive move. It lost 14.2 points and moved the score from -3.7 to +10.5 in Black's favour, the only time Black led by double digits. The engine's choice was J5. After that both sides just filled points and passed, and Black's lead stayed at about +11."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "The only program run was libs.py, which parses a board pasted on stdin and prints each group's stones and liberties; it does no move selection or lookahead. The other commands were reading and appending to games.md, a plain game log and notes file.", "stop": false}}